  "title": "The flitter today, followed for 240 frames",
  "question": "Does this read as an animal flying between flowers, or as a frog?",
  "context_md": "The lab's flying pollinator on the understory bed, `main` today, at play zoom. The magenta ring is the animal; the camera is locked onto that one flitter for the whole clip, so **the animal stays still in frame and the world slides past it** -- motion is the background moving.\n\nThis is the same build your three round-29 cards were cut from, re-shot as movement rather than a contact sheet, because all three verdicts said the same thing (\"both look very much like hopping\", \"I cannot tell from these images\", \"it is stuck next to a plant, then does one long hop\") and I want to know whether the sheets were the problem before I rebuild the flight model.\n\nWhat I already measured, so you do not have to look for it: the animal is in the air for **23 frames per take-off**, and a plain thrown arc with no wings at all lasts 22 -- so the wings are currently buying **one frame**. Over this whole 240-frame clip the world moves **once**.",
